<h2>The Dallas County Storm Calendar</h2>
<p>Hail reports in Dallas County cluster from March through June, with April and May the busiest months and a smaller second peak in October. Severe wind can arrive with any strong thunderstorm line, as it did on May 28, 2024, when the National Weather Service found winds up to 95 mph in Garland and 90 mph in Richardson. Tornadoes have struck in spring, fall, and even late December, including the EF4 that hit Garland and Rowlett on December 26, 2015. Winter brings its own risks: Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 and ice storms in February 2023, January 2025, and January 2026.</p>
<p>See our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page for a searchable record of past events.</p>

<h2>When Storm Damage Overlaps</h2>
<p>Multi-cause losses are where storm claims get complicated. A few examples:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind and rain.</strong> Interior rain damage is often covered only if wind or hail first created an opening in the roof or walls.</li>
<li><strong>Wind and flood.</strong> If a storm damages the roof and also sends water in at ground level, the homeowners policy and a flood policy may each cover part of the loss, and some policies contain language that limits coverage when flood contributes. Careful documentation of each source matters.</li>
<li><strong>Old and new damage.</strong> A roof hit by hail in one season and wind in the next may draw a &ldquo;prior damage&rdquo; argument from the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Trees.</strong> Wind or ice can bring down trees, and coverage for removal depends on whether the tree struck a covered structure and the policy&rsquo;s debris removal limits.</li>
</ul>

