<h2>Documenting a Storm Damage Claim</h2>
<p>Strong storm claims share a few things: dated photos taken before cleanup, weather records that place the storm at the property, a complete inspection inside and out, and an itemized estimate. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather. Keep receipts for tarping, board-up, water extraction, tree removal, and any hotel stays. Save damaged materials when practical until the insurer inspects. If the insurer&rsquo;s adjuster visits, walk the property with them and point out every area of damage, including the attic, fence lines, and outbuildings.</p>

<h2>Deductibles and Payment Basics</h2>
<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often 1% or 2% of the dwelling limit. Replacement cost policies typically pay actual cash value first, then release recoverable depreciation after repairs. Dallas County is not in the TWIA coastal area, so wind and hail coverage usually comes from your regular insurer. Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims law, insurers have set deadlines to respond, which are extended by 15 days after a weather catastrophe declared by the insurance commissioner.</p>

<h2>Homes and Commercial Properties</h2>
<p>Storm claims look different depending on the building. On a brick-veneer house with a composition shingle roof, the focus is often the roof, gutters, windows, fence, and any interior leaks. On apartments, offices, and warehouses with flat TPO or modified-bitumen roofs, hail and wind can damage the membrane, insulation, rooftop HVAC units, and skylights, and a leak can spread through several tenant spaces. Commercial policies may also include business income coverage and different deductibles. See <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> for details on each.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster is a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance. As a public adjuster in Dallas, we work for the policyholder on storm claims of every size, from a damaged fence to a commercial roof. We can inspect, document, prepare an Xactimate estimate, gather weather data, and communicate with your insurer. If you already have an offer, our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">estimate review</a> can show what may be missing. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>After the Storm: A Short Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Check for hazards: downed lines (report to Oncor), gas odors (report to Atmos Energy), and structural damage.</li>
